<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">PRRI rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1958">1958</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Revolutionary Government of the Republic of Indonesia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The PRRI rebellion was a regional revolt against the central government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, launched when dissident army officers and politicians proclaimed the Revolutionary Government of the Republic of Indonesia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-sumatra">West Sumatra</a> in February 1958. Opposed to the growing power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> and the communists, and to Javanese domination, the rebels joined forces with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/permesta">Permesta</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a>. Government troops took their towns within months, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> remnants surrendered under an amnesty in 1961.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prri-rebellion.jpg" alt="PRRI rebellion" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BorysMapping · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PRRI_campaign.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The central government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a> tolerated the dissident army councils of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> for almost twelve months before preparing to defeat the Revolutionary Government of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> by force. It regarded the movement as separatist. The armed forces of the republic, known as APRI and nicknamed the central army, mounted joint operations of the army, navy and air force under General Abdul Haris Nasution, whose military experience far exceeded that of the rebel leader, Lieutenant Colonel Ahmad Husein.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The government campaign opened on 12 March 1958 and quickly kept the rebels from destroying the Caltex oil fields and refinery at Pekanbaru. The large amount of American equipment abandoned by the fleeing rebels showed that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> was supplying them: the CIA had covertly backed the PRRI in 1958. Nasution launched surprise amphibious assaults on rebel cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> that March, and by May the balance had swung firmly towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>. Husein was left with withdrawal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war, and after the Pope incident of 18 May 1958 the United States turned its support back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a>.</p><p>As administration in Central Sumatra collapsed, the government divided the region into three provinces, and Kaharuddin Datuk Rangkayo Basa became the first governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-sumatra">West Sumatra</a> on 17 May 1958. Government soldiers arrested thousands of suspected sympathisers, and mass killings took place: beneath the Jam Gadang clock tower about 187 people were shot, only 17 of them rebel soldiers. Schools were closed from mid-April 1958 to 1960, and Andalas University shut because almost all its staff and students had joined the rebels. By the end of 1960 the army held all of West Sumatra.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>At the end of 1960 Nasution launched Operation Call Back to persuade rebel officers to surrender, exploiting rifts within the movement. Husein surrendered on 29 May 1961 with about 24,500 followers, and Presidential Decree No. 322 of 22 June 1961 granted an amnesty; Mohammad Natsir, the last of the leaders, gave himself up on 28 September 1961. The amnesty proved largely rhetorical, as former leaders were kept in quarantine for years. Nasution counted 7,146 dead on both sides, 6,115 of them from the rebel side, and Saafroedin Bahar judged the toll far greater than that of the war of independence against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “PRRI rebellion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 1950s it suppressed regional rebellions, including the Darul Islam movement, which sought an Islamic state, and the <b>PRRI</b> and Permesta revolts in Sumatra and Sulawesi in 1958.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
